//Override and Final

These keywords are mainly used with inheritance and virtual functions.

override

override tells the compiler that a child-class function is intended to override a virtual function from the parent.

class Animal {
public:
    virtual void sound() {
        cout << "Animal";
    }
};

class Dog : public Animal {
public:
    void sound() override {
        cout << "Bark";
    }
};

If you accidentally write the wrong function signature, override makes the compiler give an error.

final

final prevents further overriding or inheritance.

Prevent overriding:
class Animal {
public:
    virtual void sound() final {
        cout << "Animal";
    }
};

Now a child class cannot override sound().

Prevent inheritance:
class Dog final {
};

Now:

class Puppy : public Dog {
};  // ❌

is not allowed.

Simple definition

override → confirms that a function overrides a parent virtual function.
final → prevents further overriding or inheritance.